# Week 2 — Requirements Elicitation & Candidate Requirements

## 1. Stakeholder Analysis & Elicitation Plan

### 1.1 Interview Plan: Event Organiser
Goal: Understand current manual matching pain points and notification workflows
Open Questions:
How do you currently match volunteers to specific event roles
What happens when a volunteer cancels at the last minute
How do you verify if a volunteer has the required qualifications e.g., Garda vetting or first aid
Probing / Clarifying Questions:
When you say an event is "urgent", what exact criteria determines that urgency
What specific information do you need to see before approving a volunteer for a restricted task

### 1.2 Interview Plan: General & Skilled Volunteers
Goal: Understand registration barriers and notification preferences
Open Questions:
How do you currently find out about upcoming community events that need help
What issues do you encounter when an event schedule changes or is cancelled
Closed / Fact-Checking Questions:
Do you currently receive event reminders via email or messaging apps?


## 2. Stakeholder Conflicts & Resolution Analysis

Conflict Scenario: Volunteer Registration Flexibility vs. Event Organiser Preparation Time & Compliance
Volunteer Perspective: Wants last-minute sign-ups and instant registration without waiting for profile approvals
Event Organiser / Tech Specialist Perspective: Needs volunteer rosters finalized 48 hours in advance to prepare equipment and verify qualifications e.g., Garda vetting
Privacy/Security Officer Perspective: Requires strict admin sign-off before unvetted users can access event details involving vulnerable groups
Resolution Strategy:
Define two distinct role tiers: General Roles (allow sign-up up to 12 hours before) and Restricted Roles require verified qualifications and close 48 hours prior to the event



## 3. Candidate Requirements Catalogue

### 3.1 Functional Requirements 
Account & Profile: The system shall allow volunteers to register an account and select their skills, general availability, and upload qualification documents
Event Creation: The system shall allow event organisers to create events, specify required volunteer capacities, and set qualification pre-requisites for each role
Automated Verification Check: The system shall automatically prevent unverified volunteers from registering for restricted roles requiring background checks e.g., Garda vetting
Automated Notifications: The system shall automatically send email notifications to all registered volunteers within 2 minutes of an event cancellation or time change
Roster Management: The system shall allow organisers to view, confirm, and export volunteer attendance rosters for their assigned events.

### 3.2 Non-Functional Requirements 
Usability: At least 90% of first-time volunteer users shall be able to search and register for an event within 3 minutes without assistance
Performance/Response Time: Event availability searches shall return updated results in under 2 seconds during normal peak load
Security & Privacy / GDPR: Volunteer personal contact details (phone number, email) shall only be visible to the event organiser managing that specific event.
Availability: The system shall be available for volunteer registration 99.5% of the time during standard operational hours



## 4. Requirement Surgery 

### Example 1
Raw Stakeholder Statement:We need a calendar that everyone can see so people know what's happening
Analysis: The statement proposes a specific feature (calendar) without defining access rules, privacy restrictions, or update behavior.
Refined Functional Requirement :The system shall display a public schedule of upcoming open events filtered by date, location, and required skills
Refined Non-Functional Requirement:Public event schedules shall update in real-time within 5 seconds of an event status change

### Example 2
Raw Stakeholder Statement:The system should make booking volunteers super fast and easy
Analysis: Super fast and easy are subjective and un-testable terms
Refined Functional Requirement:The system shall allow an organiser to assign a qualified volunteer to an open role in no more than 3 clicks from the dashboard


## 5. Reflection 
Moving from initial discovery to formal elicitation showed that raw stakeholder statements cannot be used directly as system requirements. Turning vague requests like fast booking or "shared calendars" into clear FRs and NFRs prevents scope creep and keeps requirements testable. Solving the conflict between volunteer flexibility and organiser compliance early helped establish role-based registration rules before system modeling starts.
